@page "/about"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
    ViewData["Description"] = "Not a blog, not even a sandbox: a production test bench where experiments run on the real domain, against real traffic.";
}

<header class="page-head">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">About this site</p>
        <h1>Not a blog, not even a sandbox</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            It looks like a personal blog, and calling it a sandbox would be flattering:
            a sandbox protects you from consequences. This one runs on the real domain,
            against real traffic, with no staging copy in between.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="prose">
    <p>
        The site is where I test things before I know whether they are good ideas: a request
        log, a bot classifier, a calendar integration, analytics, a second community site. All of
        it runs on the real domain, inside the same deployment, next to the writing. The blog is
        where I write down what happened.
    </p>

    <p>
        And <strong>sandbox</strong> is not the right word either. A sandbox is a place where
        nothing you do can escape. This one has a public URL, real certificates, real visitors
        and no staging copy in between. It is a production test bench that also publishes
        articles.
    </p>

    <h2>Why not a lab</h2>

    <p>
        A local lab is clean: fake data, a hostname nobody resolves, no crawler hitting the
        endpoints at 3 a.m. That is exactly where the interesting failures do not happen.
    </p>

    <p>
        What happens here instead is DNS, certificate renewal, a reverse proxy, containers,
        volumes, a deploy pipeline and the occasional scanner that fills a public endpoint with
        garbage. A feature that works on a laptop and falls over behind a proxy has been
        previewed, not tested.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>

    <p>Five rules keep it from becoming a junk drawer.</p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>The experiment runs in production.</strong> No demo branch, no staging copy
            with mock data. If a feature is not deployed, it is a draft.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Every experiment has a delete path.</strong> The request log turns off with
            one flag: no middleware, no database, no queue. The booking API answers 503 when no
            calendar is configured, and the site starts normally. Analytics is off when the
            website id is empty. Removing an experiment must never mean rescuing the blog from it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The data has to be real.</strong> <code>/requests</code> does not show sample
            rows. It shows what the middleware wrote, and the same rows feed the map, the chart,
            the filters, the table and the CSV. If the numbers disagree, the experiment is broken
            and gets fixed that day.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Writing is the acceptance test.</strong> A feature is finished when I can
            write the post: the problem, the decision, what it cost. If I cannot explain the
            trade-off, I do not understand the feature yet.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>No dependency I cannot keep.</strong> Self-hosted beats SaaS. A file on a
            volume beats a managed database. One small server beats a cluster I would have to
            babysit.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>Removal is a valid outcome. When an experiment ends, the post stays.</p>

    <h2>One AppHost, the whole stack</h2>

    <figure>
        <img src="~/img/aspire-apphost.png" asp-append-version="true" decoding="async"
             alt="Diagram of the Aspire AppHost: Caddy in front of the site, theqult, maddalena and umami; bot-ai connected to the site over gRPC; named volumes for content, accounts, logs and models." />
        <figcaption>The Aspire AppHost: one graph for local development and for the compose file on the server.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Everything the site needs is declared in one Aspire AppHost: the projects, the containers,
        the endpoints and the volumes. In development <code>aspire run</code> starts the graph on
        this machine; <code>aspire publish</code> turns the same graph into the docker compose file
        used on the server. There is no second description of the architecture to keep in sync.
    </p>

    <p>The resources, briefly:</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Resource</th>
                <th>What it is</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>caddy</code></td>
                <td>The only published ports (80/443). TLS, layer4 and the PROXY protocol.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>site</code></td>
                <td>This application: blog, <code>/requests</code>, <code>/admin</code>, <code>/info-bot</code>, the two docs areas.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>theqult</code></td>
                <td>A second Razor Pages app: members, stay requests, references.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>maddalena</code></td>
                <td>A placeholder service; domain and TLS are already wired.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>umami</code>, <code>umami-db</code></td>
                <td>Cookieless analytics under <code>/analytics</code>, with PostgreSQL.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>bot-ai</code></td>
                <td>A Python service that labels request batches over gRPC.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        Named volumes keep state outside the images: <code>blog-data</code>,
        <code>auth-data</code>, <code>request-log-data</code>, <code>theqult-data</code>,
        <code>umami-db-data</code>, <code>botdetection-models</code> and
        <code>botdetection-data</code>; Caddy keeps its certificates in <code>caddy-data</code>
        and <code>caddy-config</code>. Rebuilding a container does not take the posts, the
        accounts or the log with it. That is the property that makes it safe to experiment in
        production.
    </p>

    <h2>One request, two records</h2>

    <figure>
        <img src="~/img/request-path.png" asp-append-version="true" decoding="async"
             alt="Diagram of a request: Caddy forwards to the site, the middleware enqueues an entry, a background writer stores it in Sperone, and /requests reads it. Separately, the browser sends a page view to Umami and PostgreSQL. The two records share no key." />
        <figcaption>One visit produces a server-side row in Sperone and an unrelated page view in Umami.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
        The path of a request is short and boring on purpose. Caddy terminates TLS and forwards
        to the site with the PROXY protocol, so the application sees the real client address
        instead of the proxy. The request-log middleware enqueues an entry and returns; the
        response does not wait for a database. A background writer flushes the queue every second
        or every 200 rows into Sperone, the embedded store that runs in the same process. The
        queue holds 8,192 entries and drops new ones when it is full, because losing a log line is
        better than slowing the site.
    </p>

    <p>
        The bot classifier is off that path too. The writer sends batches to <code>bot-ai</code>
        over gRPC and stores <code>IsBot</code>, <code>IsMaliciousURL</code>, the probability and
        the model name. If the model is down, the flags stay null and the log is written anyway.
        Nothing is blocked: the model labels, it does not decide who gets in.
    </p>

    <p>
        A visitor also produces a second, unrelated record. The Umami tracker calls
        <code>/analytics/api/send</code>, and Umami writes a page view to PostgreSQL. Sperone
        answers "what hit the server"; Umami answers "what the browser reported". There is no
        shared key between the two, and there will not be one: joining them would require an
        identifier that survives the request, which is exactly what a cookieless setup avoids.
    </p>

    <h2>What is running now</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The blog.</strong> Markdown files on a volume, no database. The home page
            renders the first five posts in full; the rest are summaries. Posts at
            <code>/blog/{slug}</code>, tags, RSS, and two sitemaps (HTML and XML). The editor is
            a vendored Toast UI instance that writes Markdown, so the content outlives the UI.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>/requests</code>.</strong> The request log made public: world map from
            an offline GeoIP database, visitors chart, country table grouped by continent,
            filters by country, IP, client id, user agent, bot and malicious URL, CSV export.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>/info-send</code> and <code>/info-bot</code>.</strong> A public endpoint
            stores whatever raw body a bot posts; a public page shows it back. An experiment in
            watching automated traffic talk when it thinks nobody reads it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>/admin</code>.</strong> Local accounts in SQLite, PBKDF2, no external
            identity provider.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>/visualmcp</code> and <code>/predictive-coding</code>.</strong> Two
            documentation areas rendered by the same app from two projects I work on.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>theqult.org</code>.</strong> The hospitality community, a second
            application in the same solution.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>/booking</code>.</strong> A week view over CalDAV, currently parked:
            without a calendar backend the API answers 503 and the rest of the site does not care.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>

    <p>
        It is not a portfolio: there is no grid of logos, and the projects are visible by using
        the site.
    </p>

    <p>
        It is not a product: no signups, no tracking cookies, no growth metrics, no newsletter
        popup.
    </p>

    <p>It is not a news feed: posts appear when an experiment ends, not on a schedule.</p>

    <p>
        It is not permanent: features can be removed, endpoints can return 503, pages can change.
        The archive is the record; the code is allowed to move.
    </p>

    <p>
        There is even a <a asp-page="/WhoIAmNot">page listing the other Matteo Fabbris</a>, so
        nobody credits me with their achievements. That is the level of branding ambition
        involved.
    </p>

    <h2>Why write it down</h2>

    <p>
        Because a running system is not an explanation. The post is where a decision becomes
        legible: what I tried, what broke, what I removed. It is also the only changelog that
        survives a rewrite of the code.
    </p>

    <p>
        If an experiment is worth keeping, it earns a post. If it is not, it earns a deletion.
        Either way, the site gets more interesting to run.
    </p>
</div>
